          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of John Steinbeck</strong><br>\nJohn Steinbeck was born in 1902 in Salinas, California. Growing up in a fertile agricultural valley, his experiences among the rural working class would later influence much of his work. He attended Stanford University intermittently before moving to New York City to pursue writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Steinbeck's Literary Beginnings</strong><br>\nSteinbeck began his writing career with the novel \"Cup of Gold\" in 1929. Although not a commercial success, it set the stage for his lifelong themes of social justice and the human condition. He worked various jobs to support himself while honing his craft as a writer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Great Depression and Steinbeck's Work</strong><br>\nDuring the Great Depression, Steinbeck's novels \"The Grapes of Wrath\" and \"Of Mice and Men\" highlighted the plight of migrant workers in California. \"The Grapes of Wrath,\" published in 1939, won a Pulitzer Prize and became Steinbeck's most famous work.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
