          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Steinbeck's Style and Influence</strong><br>\nSteinbeck's writing is known for its realistic and sympathetic portrayal of the struggles of ordinary people. His use of simple language made his books accessible to a wide audience. He often wrote about the poor and dispossessed, which garnered both acclaim and criticism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Views and Controversy</strong><br>\nSteinbeck's political views were shaped by his experiences with poverty and his sympathy for the working class. This led to criticism during the McCarthy era, with some claiming his work carried socialist sympathies. Despite this, he remained a popular and influential figure.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"East of Eden\" and Later Works</strong><br>\nEast of Eden, published in 1952, explored themes of destiny and sin using a multi-generational narrative. This novel, considered one of his best, demonstrated Steinbeck's growth as a writer and his ability to weave complex stories with deep moral questions.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
