            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Steinbeck's Nobel Prize</strong><br>\nIn 1962, Steinbeck was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature for his \"realistic and imaginative writings, combining as they do sympathetic humour and keen social perception.\" The award recognized the impact of his extensive body of work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nSteinbeck was married three times and had two sons. His personal life, like his professional one, was filled with both triumphs and challenges. Steinbeck's legacy lives on through his contributions to literature and his insightful examinations of the human spirit.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nSteinbeck passed away in New York City in 1968. His work continues to be celebrated and studied, with \"The Grapes of Wrath\" and \"Of Mice and Men\" regularly taught in schools across the world. Posthumously, his work still resonates with readers and critics alike.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
